  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on self-reported data from an online travel survey, which may introduce selection bias regarding who reports their cycling habits.",
      "what": "Using an online travel survey of 2,690 respondents in Vancouver, Toronto, and Montréal, this study analyzed the use of 46 cycling facilities built between 2020 and 2022. Bayesian cross-classified multilevel models revealed that each additional kilometer of residential distance from a facility reduces the odds of using it by 24%. This distance decay is significantly steeper for women, gender-diverse individuals, and lower-income riders, while older adults and women also show lower overall odds of use.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that physical proximity to new cycling infrastructure is a more critical barrier for historically underrepresented groups than for higher-income men. This provides empirical evidence that simply building equal amounts of infrastructure does not guarantee equitable access or usage across different demographic groups."
    }
  },
